    "interpretation": "At N=4 (4-way identification, chance=0.25), the smallest true accuracy detectable at alpha=0.05/power=0.80 with only 4 independent identification trials is very high (0.832 vs chance 0.25), i.e. the design can only detect near-perfect individuation, not graded effects. Doubling to N=8 tightens this substantially (0.503 vs chance 0.125), but even N=8 remains underpowered for anything but a large true effect. This is a power problem, not evidence against a real individuation 'principle' -- the study is simply not equipped to distinguish a true 100% individuation architecture from a true 60% one."
